WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:03.480
Before getting started, how many of you
have heard of

00:00:17.230 --> 00:00:22.460
Zappos prior to this, and how many of you
have shopped with us before?

00:00:23.720 --> 00:00:24.710
Wow, very cool.

00:00:24.710 --> 00:00:27.200
So normally when I do this survey to a
random group of

00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:30.270
people the ratio is actually about two to
one, women to men.

00:00:30.270 --> 00:00:34.670
And a lot of women I've asked or, or men
that I've ask say

00:00:34.670 --> 00:00:36.610
they, they haven't personally shopped from
us,

00:00:36.610 --> 00:00:38.520
but their wife or significant other has.

00:00:38.520 --> 00:00:41.540
And a lot of times it's the significant
other buying for the guy.

00:00:41.540 --> 00:00:45.470
And I was actually giving a tour in Las
Vegas little whi, a

00:00:45.470 --> 00:00:50.570
few years ago to an executive from one of
the major record labels.

00:00:50.570 --> 00:00:53.050
And I'll let, we actually were based out
of Las Vegas.

00:00:53.050 --> 00:00:56.180
So next time any of you are in Las Vegas,
we definitely recommend coming for tours.

00:00:56.180 --> 00:00:57.640
It's actually a lot of fun.

00:00:57.640 --> 00:01:00.630
We'll pick you up from the airport in a
Zappos shuttle, give you a tour.

00:01:00.630 --> 00:01:04.490
It takes about a hour, and then drop you
off at your hotel afterwards.

00:01:04.490 --> 00:01:07.400
So anyways, we were doing this for an
executive from one of

00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:10.250
the major record labels, and I happened to
be giving the tour.

00:01:10.250 --> 00:01:12.850
And I asked him the exact same question,
had you shop from us before?

00:01:12.850 --> 00:01:15.830
And he said no he hadn't but he suspects
his wife

00:01:15.830 --> 00:01:19.290
has because these white boxes would show
up on his doorstep and

00:01:19.290 --> 00:01:22.330
then they'd disappear and he didn't know
what was going on, [LAUGH]

00:01:22.330 --> 00:01:26.770
you know, if she was buying them or
exchanging them, returning them.

00:01:26.770 --> 00:01:28.870
And every time he asked his wife she would

00:01:28.870 --> 00:01:31.310
just change the subject or refuse to
answer him.

00:01:31.310 --> 00:01:36.010
And so you know, giving the tour
downstairs is the merchandising area

00:01:36.010 --> 00:01:39.220
and we walk upstairs to our Customer
Loyalty Team which is our

00:01:39.220 --> 00:01:43.700
name for our call center and as we're
walking through like I,

00:01:43.700 --> 00:01:47.310
I starting to explain stuff and then I
turn around and he's gone.

00:01:47.310 --> 00:01:48.900
And I'm like what happened?

00:01:48.900 --> 00:01:52.610
And I find out that he actually went down
one of the isles and sat

00:01:52.610 --> 00:01:58.033
down next to one of our reps and forced
her to pull up his wife's account.

00:01:58.033 --> 00:02:02.164
[LAUGH] And so as it turned out he
discovered, she

00:02:02.164 --> 00:02:07.136
had spent over $62,000 in her life time so
[NOISE].

00:02:07.136 --> 00:02:14.770
Yeah [LAUGH] hopefully we weren't
instrumental in any divorce proceedings.

00:02:14.770 --> 00:02:21.570
so, and then, as was mentioned, I have a
book that came out a few months ago called

00:02:21.570 --> 00:02:26.620
Delivering Happiness and we're actually on
this three month cross country bus tour.

00:02:26.620 --> 00:02:31.450
We got this 50 foot bus, and actually we
have some pictures to, to show you.

00:02:31.450 --> 00:02:32.520
We wrapped the bus.

00:02:32.520 --> 00:02:37.050
There's ten of us on the bus, and we're,
we've gone through ten cities so far.

00:02:37.050 --> 00:02:41.340
And and we, and the inside, I don't think
I have any inside pictures of us.

00:02:41.340 --> 00:02:43.970
But this is the Delivering Happiness Book
Team

00:02:43.970 --> 00:02:45.780
and it's actually its own mini start up.

00:02:45.780 --> 00:02:50.180
There's about 20 of us and 10 of us are on
the bus at any one point.

00:02:50.180 --> 00:02:51.370
So if you want to find out more

00:02:51.370 --> 00:02:52.185
information, just go to

00:02:52.185 --> 00:02:55.531
deliveringhappinessbus.com and also
deliveringhappinessbook.com.

00:02:55.531 --> 00:02:58.970
There is fun videos and pictures and, and
so on there.

00:02:58.970 --> 00:03:02.640
And and, oh yeah, this is also a fun video
to, to watch.

00:03:02.640 --> 00:03:03.400
If you have the time.

00:03:03.400 --> 00:03:07.280
And I'll make this presentation available,
so you can check out the video later.

00:03:07.280 --> 00:03:11.020
But we're actually, the bus is in Miami
right now, so I'll

00:03:11.020 --> 00:03:14.300
be meeting back up with the bus at the end
of this week.

00:03:14.300 --> 00:03:18.429
But before getting into Zappos, I wanted
to talk about what led me to Zappos.

00:03:19.430 --> 00:03:24.140
And the story actually begins in college
with pizza.

00:03:24.140 --> 00:03:27.220
I was running a pizza business on the
ground in from of my dorm in

00:03:27.220 --> 00:03:32.940
college and with my roommates Sanji, He
and I decided to invest in pizza ovens.

00:03:32.940 --> 00:03:34.650
And it's this area that was, we set aside,

00:03:34.650 --> 00:03:37.880
we probably had 300 or 400 students in our
dorm.

00:03:37.880 --> 00:03:40.630
And the way it would work is every year
there would be a new

00:03:40.630 --> 00:03:45.280
set of students that would run that grill,
it was called the grill area downstairs.

00:03:45.280 --> 00:03:48.650
And so one year we had, we were the
highest bidders and we

00:03:48.650 --> 00:03:52.210
bought it from the owners from the
previous year and we decided to

00:03:52.210 --> 00:03:56.780
invest in pizza ovens, and we, hired other
students, set the menus, dealt

00:03:56.780 --> 00:04:01.930
with suppliers and so on and occasionally
I was making the pizzas myself.

00:04:01.930 --> 00:04:06.775
And this guy named Alfred who actually is
the Chief Financial Officer today at

00:04:06.775 --> 00:04:12.200
Zappos, he would stop by every night and
order a large pepperoni pizza from me.

00:04:12.200 --> 00:04:15.720
And this is actually how we meet, and to
me actually it wasn't that weird.

00:04:15.720 --> 00:04:19.330
He was doing it cause I had heard about
his reputation and

00:04:19.330 --> 00:04:22.950
he was known to actually eat it, really
eat a lot of food.

00:04:22.950 --> 00:04:26.610
He had nicknames like monster or human
trash compactor.

00:04:26.610 --> 00:04:29.870
And literally there would be nights when
there would be 10 of us

00:04:29.870 --> 00:04:33.810
late night at a Chinese restaurant and he
would just finish everyone's leftovers.

00:04:33.810 --> 00:04:37.640
So you know, not that weird but sometimes
a few hours later he'd come by and

00:04:37.640 --> 00:04:39.980
order another large pepperoni pizza from
me and

00:04:39.980 --> 00:04:43.160
I was like wow this boy can really eat.

00:04:43.160 --> 00:04:46.820
Well I found out several years later
Alfred was taking the

00:04:46.820 --> 00:04:51.030
pizza's up stairs and selling them off by
the slice so.

00:04:53.840 --> 00:04:56.200
That's why he's our chief financial
officer.

00:04:56.200 --> 00:05:02.130
So after the pizza business, then Sanji
and I, who I was running

00:05:02.130 --> 00:05:05.530
the pizza business with, we got together
and formed a company called link exchange.

00:05:05.530 --> 00:05:08.920
And this is during the first .com
craziness,

00:05:08.920 --> 00:05:10.380
and we grew that to about 100 or

00:05:10.380 --> 00:05:12.400
so people and then ended up selling the

00:05:12.400 --> 00:05:15.280
company to Microsoft two and a half years
later.

00:05:15.280 --> 00:05:19.650
But what a lot of people don't know is the
real reason why we ended up selling

00:05:19.650 --> 00:05:22.900
the company, and the real reason is
because it

00:05:22.900 --> 00:05:25.810
just wasn't a fun place to work at
anymore.

00:05:25.810 --> 00:05:28.650
And the company culture just went
completely downhill.

00:05:28.650 --> 00:05:30.300
I remember when it was just five or ten of
us.

00:05:30.300 --> 00:05:32.690
It was your typical .com is a lot of fun.

00:05:32.690 --> 00:05:35.040
We were working around the clock, sleeping
under our desks.

00:05:35.040 --> 00:05:37.220
Had no idea what day of the week it was.

00:05:37.220 --> 00:05:40.120
Trying to remind ourselves to shower
occasionally but

00:05:40.120 --> 00:05:41.940
lots of fun and as we were growing

00:05:41.940 --> 00:05:44.370
we hired friends and friends of friends
and

00:05:44.370 --> 00:05:46.230
I remember there was a friend of mine that

00:05:46.230 --> 00:05:51.090
was on a cross country trip from New York
and this was all out of our

00:05:51.090 --> 00:05:56.420
apartment here in San Mateo at the time
and he stopped by to help out and then.

00:05:56.420 --> 00:05:59.740
He actually never made his way back home,
he ended up joining the

00:05:59.740 --> 00:06:04.870
company, and that works really well until
we got to about 20 people.

00:06:04.870 --> 00:06:08.890
We were having a lot of fun, and then we
ran into a really big problem

00:06:08.890 --> 00:06:13.370
when we got to 20 people, and the problem
is basically we ran out of friends.

00:06:13.370 --> 00:06:15.630
And so we're trying to figure out, okay
what do

00:06:15.630 --> 00:06:18.200
we do now, we didn't know how to hire
people.

00:06:18.200 --> 00:06:20.690
And so we started just figuring out on our

00:06:20.690 --> 00:06:22.640
own, and we ended up hiring actually all
the

00:06:22.640 --> 00:06:25.130
people with the right skill sets and
experience, but

00:06:25.130 --> 00:06:28.940
they weren't all necessarily great for the
company culture.

00:06:28.940 --> 00:06:33.660
And by the time we got to 100 people, I
myself dreaded getting out

00:06:33.660 --> 00:06:37.190
of the bed in the morning to go to the
office and that's kind of

00:06:37.190 --> 00:06:41.030
a weird feeling cuz this was a company I
co-founded and I felt like,

00:06:41.030 --> 00:06:44.420
you know, if I felt that way, how much did
all the other employees feel.

00:06:44.420 --> 00:06:48.570
So that's really what led us to sell the
company to Microsoft.

00:06:48.570 --> 00:06:51.560
And after the sale, myself, Sanji, and
most of

00:06:51.560 --> 00:06:55.710
the early employees, we all left the
company shortly thereafter.

00:06:55.710 --> 00:06:59.730
So after that sale, you know, worked out
well financially trying to figure out,

00:06:59.730 --> 00:07:02.440
okay, now what do I want, what do I want
to do with my life?

00:07:02.440 --> 00:07:04.780
And I'm the type of person who gets

00:07:04.780 --> 00:07:08.710
bored real easily and always excited by
new ideas.

00:07:08.710 --> 00:07:11.390
And so Alfred and I got together and we
formed a fund.

00:07:11.390 --> 00:07:14.430
And invest in about 20 or so different
internet companies.

00:07:14.430 --> 00:07:17.370
Then in my mind I was like wow, this is a
great way to

00:07:17.370 --> 00:07:20.960
really just get exposed to a lot of
different businesses and ideas and so on.

00:07:20.960 --> 00:07:23.545
And making investments was actually pretty
exciting and pretty

00:07:23.545 --> 00:07:26.260
fun.There was always some new idea, some
new business.

00:07:26.260 --> 00:07:29.600
And you know this was in the beginning of
the internet days.

00:07:29.600 --> 00:07:32.020
But then after we had made all the
investments and

00:07:32.020 --> 00:07:35.150
you know, there wasn't anymore money to
invest in the fund.

00:07:35.150 --> 00:07:40.920
I realized that at that point and for me
at least investing was pretty boring.

00:07:40.920 --> 00:07:42.930
I felt like I was sitting on the sidelines

00:07:42.930 --> 00:07:46.800
and I really missed being part of building
something.

00:07:46.800 --> 00:07:49.670
So I started thinking about what do I
wanna do and out of

00:07:49.670 --> 00:07:52.750
all the companies who had invested in,
Zappos happened to be one of them.

00:07:52.750 --> 00:07:55.100
And it was both the most exciting and the
most promising.

00:07:55.100 --> 00:07:57.100
So basically within a year I joined Zappos

00:07:57.100 --> 00:08:00.900
full time and, I've been with Zappos ever
since.

00:08:00.900 --> 00:08:02.410
Some of you may have heard, it was about

00:08:02.410 --> 00:08:06.160
a year ago that Amazon announced they were
acquiring Zappos.

00:08:06.160 --> 00:08:08.530
And, except, it's actually very different
from most

00:08:08.530 --> 00:08:11.750
acquisitions that, Amazon has done, in, in
the past.

00:08:11.750 --> 00:08:14.130
In most of their acquisitions, the plan is
to

00:08:14.130 --> 00:08:17.230
integrate the company being acquired into
the parent company, and

00:08:17.230 --> 00:08:22.000
then eventually, the, original company
kind of loses its identity

00:08:22.000 --> 00:08:26.360
and everything's integrated into the
mother ship and absorbed there.

00:08:26.360 --> 00:08:29.790
Whereas for us, as a precondition for even
considering to explore

00:08:29.790 --> 00:08:33.370
the deal, we told them that we would only
consider it if

00:08:33.370 --> 00:08:37.030
Zappos could remain independent, if we
could continue to grow our

00:08:37.030 --> 00:08:41.350
brand, and our culture and our way of
doing business, our way.

00:08:41.350 --> 00:08:43.980
And the great news is they've remained
true to their word.

00:08:43.980 --> 00:08:46.370
So from our point of view it's just as if

00:08:46.370 --> 00:08:48.400
we've swapped out our Board of Directors
for a new one.

00:08:48.400 --> 00:08:51.610
So once a quarter instead of flying to the
Bay Area we now

00:08:51.610 --> 00:08:55.440
fly to Seattle once a quarter for the
equivalent of a board meeting.

00:08:55.440 --> 00:08:58.450
So most people, when they hear about
Zappos, think of us

00:08:58.450 --> 00:09:02.240
as an online retailer of shoes because
that's how we started.

00:09:02.240 --> 00:09:05.410
But internally, we actually have a
different, mentality.

00:09:05.410 --> 00:09:09.470
We have a saying that we're a service
company, that just happens to sell shoes.

00:09:09.470 --> 00:09:11.110
And in fact we sell a lot more than shoes
today.

00:09:11.110 --> 00:09:13.320
We sell clothing, beauty products,
housewares,

00:09:13.320 --> 00:09:15.550
kitchenwares, and, and, and so on.

00:09:15.550 --> 00:09:18.150
And we're actually hoping ten years from
now, people

00:09:18.150 --> 00:09:20.510
won't even realize we started out selling
shoes online.

00:09:20.510 --> 00:09:22.220
We really just wanna build the Zappos
brand to

00:09:22.220 --> 00:09:26.170
be about the very best customer service
and customer experience.

00:09:26.170 --> 00:09:28.350
And in fact it doesn't even have to be
limited to online.

00:09:28.350 --> 00:09:31.030
We've actually had customers email us and
ask us if

00:09:31.030 --> 00:09:34.840
we would please start an airline or run
the IRS.

00:09:34.840 --> 00:09:41.010
We're not gonna do either of those things
this year, but 20 or 30 years from now

00:09:41.010 --> 00:09:43.480
I wouldn't rule out a Zappos airlines,
that's just

00:09:43.480 --> 00:09:46.830
about the very best customer service and
customer experience.

00:09:46.830 --> 00:09:50.540
So one brand that we look to for
inspiration sometimes is Virgin.

00:09:50.540 --> 00:09:54.670
They're in a whole bunch of businesses,
they're in music, airlines and so on.

00:09:54.670 --> 00:09:57.530
The difference is the Virgin brand is more
about being hip and

00:09:57.530 --> 00:10:01.320
cool where as we just wanna be about the
very best customer service.

00:10:01.320 --> 00:10:04.130
So our whole philosophy is let's take most
of the money

00:10:04.130 --> 00:10:07.850
that we would have spend on paid
advertising or paid marketing.

00:10:07.850 --> 00:10:11.850
And rather than buy our exposure, instead,
let's

00:10:11.850 --> 00:10:15.210
invest it into customer service, and the
customer experience.

00:10:15.210 --> 00:10:18.890
And let our customers do the marketing for
us through word of mouth.

00:10:18.890 --> 00:10:22.000
On any given day about 75% of our orders
are from

00:10:22.000 --> 00:10:25.610
repeat customers and we basically grew
from no sales in 1999.

00:10:25.610 --> 00:10:29.260
In 2008, was the first year where we hit a
Billion Dollars

00:10:29.260 --> 00:10:33.390
in gross merchandise sales, and actually,
even despite the bad economy, we've

00:10:33.390 --> 00:10:36.790
actually continued to grow over the last
two years, in fact our

00:10:36.790 --> 00:10:40.790
net sales for Q1 this year were up almost
50% year over year.

00:10:40.790 --> 00:10:44.840
And people ask us, what do we do
differently over this last 24 months?

00:10:44.840 --> 00:10:46.940
And, it's actually, we actually didn't do
anything differently.

00:10:46.940 --> 00:10:50.290
And it's not because of anything we did
over the past two years.

00:10:50.290 --> 00:10:52.950
It's because of all the investment we made
prior to that,

00:10:52.950 --> 00:10:56.560
that we continue to grow because of the
loyalty of our customers.

00:10:56.560 --> 00:10:57.840
And the number one driver of all that

00:10:57.840 --> 00:11:00.960
growth is through repeat customers and
word of mouth.

00:11:02.040 --> 00:11:04.700
So these are some of the questions that we
ask ourselves

00:11:04.700 --> 00:11:08.260
in terms of thinking about how to deliver
great customer service.

00:11:08.260 --> 00:11:10.540
And we internally use the word wow

00:11:10.540 --> 00:11:13.490
when thinking about how you would treat
customers.

00:11:13.490 --> 00:11:14.630
But we use it as a verb.

00:11:14.630 --> 00:11:17.170
We, we talk about how do we wow our
customers and also

00:11:17.170 --> 00:11:20.540
how do we wow our employees and the
vendors that we work with.

00:11:20.540 --> 00:11:23.400
And it starts with the policies you see on
our website.

00:11:23.400 --> 00:11:25.420
We offer free shipping both ways.

00:11:25.420 --> 00:11:27.950
So, a lot of people will order 10
different pairs of

00:11:27.950 --> 00:11:30.660
shoes, try them on in the comfort of their
living room with

00:11:30.660 --> 00:11:33.070
10 different outfits, and then send back
the ones that don't

00:11:33.070 --> 00:11:37.280
fit or they don't like, and we encourage
that type of behavior.

00:11:37.280 --> 00:11:40.630
We have a 365 day return policy, for
people that,

00:11:40.630 --> 00:11:42.993
I guess, have trouble, committing or
making up their minds,

00:11:42.993 --> 00:11:43.581
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:11:43.581 --> 00:11:46.432
&gt;&gt; And you know, they can take a while to
to return

00:11:46.432 --> 00:11:50.760
products and you know, most websites it's
very hard to find contact information.

00:11:50.760 --> 00:11:54.230
Usually, it's buried five links deep and
maybe

00:11:54.230 --> 00:11:56.680
it's an e-mail address you can only e-mail
once.

00:11:56.680 --> 00:11:59.540
Whereas for us, we take the exact opposite
approach.

00:11:59.540 --> 00:12:03.530
We put our 1-800 number on the top of
every single page of our website.

00:12:03.530 --> 00:12:06.850
Because we actually want to talk to our
customers.

00:12:06.850 --> 00:12:08.880
And it is funny because sometimes I will
be

00:12:08.880 --> 00:12:12.150
speaking at a branding or marketing
conference and there

00:12:12.150 --> 00:12:14.470
is a lot of discussion about consumers
being bombarded

00:12:14.470 --> 00:12:17.050
with thousand and thousand of marketing
messages every day.

00:12:17.050 --> 00:12:18.970
How do you get your message to stand out,
how do

00:12:18.970 --> 00:12:21.730
you get your brand to stand out and should
they be investing

00:12:21.730 --> 00:12:24.910
millions of dollars into this million
dollar Super Bowl ad that

00:12:24.910 --> 00:12:27.650
people may or may not be paying attention
to or should they.

00:12:27.650 --> 00:12:30.650
&gt;&gt; Be it leveraging the latest, the social
media

00:12:30.650 --> 00:12:34.550
fad, and for us as kind of low tech and

00:12:34.550 --> 00:12:37.830
unsexy as it may sound, we found that the
telephone

00:12:37.830 --> 00:12:40.390
is actually one of the best branding
devices out there.

00:12:40.390 --> 00:12:43.760
Cuz we have the customer's undivided
attention for five

00:12:43.760 --> 00:12:46.750
to ten minutes and if we get the
interaction right,

00:12:46.750 --> 00:12:48.810
we find that the customers remember that
for a very

00:12:48.810 --> 00:12:52.640
long time and tell their friends and
family about us.

00:12:52.640 --> 00:12:55.340
And we run our call center very
differently from most call centers.

00:12:55.340 --> 00:12:59.160
We don't have scripts, we don't have this
concept

00:12:59.160 --> 00:13:01.290
of average handle time which most call
centers have.

00:13:01.290 --> 00:13:03.480
Which is all about how quickly can you get
the

00:13:03.480 --> 00:13:06.310
customer off the phone in the name of
being more efficient.

00:13:06.310 --> 00:13:08.150
But we're not trying to maximize for
efficiency.

00:13:08.150 --> 00:13:11.800
We're trying maximize for the customer
experience.

00:13:11.800 --> 00:13:15.090
And we don't up sell and so going back to
the no, no call time.

00:13:15.090 --> 00:13:17.150
Actually I just got an email last night.

00:13:17.150 --> 00:13:19.750
A new record was set for the longest phone
call ever.

00:13:19.750 --> 00:13:22.680
It was eight hours and three minutes long.

00:13:22.680 --> 00:13:28.100
So, yeah, I don't know how the bathroom
situation worked out for, for that one.

00:13:28.100 --> 00:13:28.600
But,

00:13:31.020 --> 00:13:35.510
so, and it may seem weird that, you know,
for us,

00:13:35.510 --> 00:13:38.560
we're an Internet company, 95% of our
sales go through the website.

00:13:38.560 --> 00:13:41.490
So why do we focus so much on the
telephone?

00:13:41.490 --> 00:13:45.250
And what we found is actually on average
almost every

00:13:45.250 --> 00:13:49.210
customer calls us at least once sometime
during their lifetime.

00:13:49.210 --> 00:13:52.260
And it's actually usually not to place an
order.

00:13:52.260 --> 00:13:54.380
Most of our phone calls do not result in
orders.

00:13:54.380 --> 00:13:58.030
It might be their first time going through
the returns process and they

00:13:58.030 --> 00:14:02.200
just need help stepping through the
process of printing out the return label.

00:14:02.200 --> 00:14:04.650
For the first time or maybe they have a
wedding

00:14:04.650 --> 00:14:07.900
over the weekend and they just want some
fashion advice and

00:14:07.900 --> 00:14:10.170
I think we have some customers that call
us because

00:14:10.170 --> 00:14:15.370
they're lonely [LAUGH] and we'll we'll
talk to them as well.

00:14:15.370 --> 00:14:19.750
So, so that's our call center and then for
our warehouse,

00:14:19.750 --> 00:14:23.020
we actually run it very differently from
most warehouses as well.

00:14:23.020 --> 00:14:28.190
Most warehouses they let the orders pile
up so that when, the picker needs

00:14:28.190 --> 00:14:30.210
to walk around, there's higher picking
density,

00:14:30.210 --> 00:14:32.550
more efficient, doesn't need to walk as
far.

00:14:32.550 --> 00:14:34.870
For us we run our warehouse 24 seven which

00:14:34.870 --> 00:14:37.618
is not the most efficient way to run a
warehouse.

00:14:37.618 --> 00:14:41.010
and, but we're not trying to maximize for
efficiency.

00:14:41.010 --> 00:14:44.180
And because we run it 24/7, and because
our warehouse

00:14:44.180 --> 00:14:46.961
is located in Kentucky, right next to the
UPS hub,

00:14:46.961 --> 00:14:50.456
and because we do these surprise upgrades
to overnight shipping

00:14:50.456 --> 00:14:53.202
for a lot of, most of our repeat loyal
customers.

00:14:53.202 --> 00:14:55.729
When you combine all three of those
things, a

00:14:55.729 --> 00:14:58.933
lot of customers order as late as midnight
eastern time.

00:14:58.933 --> 00:15:00.506
And the shoes show up on their door step

00:15:00.506 --> 00:15:02.972
eight hours later when they're expecting
it a week later.

00:15:02.972 --> 00:15:05.196
And that creates that whole emotional
response

00:15:05.196 --> 00:15:07.820
we're trying to elicit and that wow
experience.

00:15:07.820 --> 00:15:09.580
That causes them to remember us for a very

00:15:09.580 --> 00:15:12.580
long time and tell their friends and
family about us.

00:15:12.580 --> 00:15:15.800
So for all this focus on customer service
and building our brand to be

00:15:15.800 --> 00:15:19.160
about customer service, customer service
is actually

00:15:19.160 --> 00:15:21.636
not the number one priority of the
company.

00:15:21.636 --> 00:15:25.190
Our number one priority is company
culture.

00:15:25.190 --> 00:15:26.600
And our whole belief is that, if you get

00:15:26.600 --> 00:15:29.590
the culture right, most of the other stuff
like

00:15:29.590 --> 00:15:32.820
delivering great customer service or
building long, a long-term

00:15:32.820 --> 00:15:37.130
enduring brand will just happen naturally
on its own.

00:15:37.130 --> 00:15:39.910
And our belief is that a company's culture
and a

00:15:39.910 --> 00:15:43.370
company's brand are really just two sides
of the same coin.

00:15:43.370 --> 00:15:47.100
The brand is just a lagging indicator of
the culture and

00:15:47.100 --> 00:15:49.130
if you think of, you know, ask random
people about what they

00:15:49.130 --> 00:15:52.400
think of the airline industry as whole and
usually you'll get

00:15:52.400 --> 00:15:57.040
back responses about bad customer service
or apathetic employees and so on.

00:15:57.040 --> 00:16:00.880
And like it or not that is the brand of
the industry,

00:16:00.880 --> 00:16:03.640
even though no airline set out for that to
be their brand.

00:16:03.640 --> 00:16:06.290
So we really focus on getting the culture
right.

00:16:06.290 --> 00:16:10.480
So what are some of the things that we do
to really help build our culture?

00:16:10.480 --> 00:16:13.240
Well, it starts with the hiring process.

00:16:13.240 --> 00:16:15.400
Everyone that's hired in our headquarters
in Las Vegas, it

00:16:15.400 --> 00:16:19.120
doesn't matter what position, you know,
accountant, lawyer, software developer.

00:16:19.120 --> 00:16:23.020
You go through the exact same training as
our call center reps.

00:16:23.020 --> 00:16:24.920
One of the weeks is actually later on when
we send

00:16:24.920 --> 00:16:28.020
you to Kentucky and you do all the
different warehouse functions.

00:16:28.020 --> 00:16:30.790
Picking, packing, shipping, receiving, and
so on.

00:16:30.790 --> 00:16:32.310
But the first four weeks before you start

00:16:32.310 --> 00:16:34.840
your actual job, we do call center
training.

00:16:34.840 --> 00:16:37.140
And we go over company history, the
importance

00:16:37.140 --> 00:16:40.500
of company culture, our philosophy, our
customer service.

00:16:40.500 --> 00:16:44.400
And then you're actually on the phone for
two weeks taking calls from customers.

00:16:44.400 --> 00:16:46.830
And the reason why we have everyone do
that

00:16:46.830 --> 00:16:49.920
is because if we're serious about building
our brand

00:16:49.920 --> 00:16:51.950
to be about the very best customer service
and

00:16:51.950 --> 00:16:56.170
customer experience, then customer service
shouldn't just be a department.

00:16:56.170 --> 00:16:58.150
It should be the entire company.

00:16:58.150 --> 00:17:00.354
And the other great thing about that is
during our

00:17:00.354 --> 00:17:03.148
busy Q four holiday season which is now
coming up, people

00:17:03.148 --> 00:17:05.568
from all different departments can and do
help out and

00:17:05.568 --> 00:17:08.370
they hop on the phones and help answer the
phone calls.

00:17:08.370 --> 00:17:11.670
So that means we don't need to hire temps
that may be bad for our culture.

00:17:11.670 --> 00:17:13.270
Where you don't need to hire temps that
may not

00:17:13.270 --> 00:17:17.210
deliver the same level of customer service
that we're looking for.

00:17:17.210 --> 00:17:22.960
The other thing that we do is at, at the
end of the training we actually

00:17:22.960 --> 00:17:28.660
make an offer to the entire class and the
offer is this.

00:17:28.660 --> 00:17:31.640
We will pay you actually it starts at the
beginning

00:17:31.640 --> 00:17:33.750
of the end of the first week of training,
they've

00:17:33.750 --> 00:17:35.560
been there for a week, and the offer is we

00:17:35.560 --> 00:17:38.380
will pay you for that week you spend
training, plus

00:17:38.380 --> 00:17:41.770
a bonus of $2000 to quit and leave the
company

00:17:41.770 --> 00:17:44.940
right now, and that's a standing offer
until the end

00:17:44.940 --> 00:17:47.720
of the training, and then we raise it to
$3000,

00:17:47.720 --> 00:17:51.210
and extend it a couple months beyond that
as well.

00:17:51.210 --> 00:17:54.530
And the reason we initially did this was
because we

00:17:54.530 --> 00:17:58.240
didn't want employees that were there just
for a paycheck.

00:17:58.240 --> 00:18:01.480
And starting pay in Las Vegas is $11 an
hour, there's

00:18:01.480 --> 00:18:05.510
plenty of other call centers there and so
it's a pretty

00:18:05.510 --> 00:18:09.070
significant amount of money, and on
average we found that actually

00:18:09.070 --> 00:18:11.140
about 2 or 3% of people end up taking the
offer.

00:18:11.140 --> 00:18:12.910
The offer started several years ago at a
$100.

00:18:12.910 --> 00:18:14.930
And we actually keep upping the offer
because we

00:18:14.930 --> 00:18:17.670
feel like not enough people are taking the
offer.

00:18:17.670 --> 00:18:24.230
But what surprised us and what we didn't
realize was that actually

00:18:24.230 --> 00:18:28.070
the biggest benefit came from the people
who didn't take the offer

00:18:28.070 --> 00:18:31.150
because they still had to go home, talk to
their friends and

00:18:31.150 --> 00:18:35.940
family and ask themselves, is this a
company that I really believe in?

00:18:35.940 --> 00:18:37.910
Is this a company that I want to be a

00:18:37.910 --> 00:18:41.990
part of and whose culture I want to
contribute to.

00:18:41.990 --> 00:18:45.000
And when they decide to turn down the easy
money, what we found

00:18:45.000 --> 00:18:47.390
is that, when they're back in the office
on Monday, that they're that

00:18:47.390 --> 00:18:52.340
much more passionate and engaged and, and
and, and really that, by far

00:18:52.340 --> 00:18:56.320
that's been the biggest benefit for us
that we didn't expect in doing that.

00:18:56.320 --> 00:19:00.080
But before someone gets started at Zappos,
before they go through

00:19:00.080 --> 00:19:03.190
the training, we actually interview
everyone

00:19:03.190 --> 00:19:05.620
in two sets of separate interviews.

00:19:05.620 --> 00:19:07.770
The first set of interviews for someone is

00:19:07.770 --> 00:19:09.560
kind of the standard of the hiring manager
and

00:19:09.560 --> 00:19:12.000
his or her team will interview for fit
within

00:19:12.000 --> 00:19:15.260
the team, relevant experience, technical
ability and so on.

00:19:15.260 --> 00:19:19.630
But the second set of interviews is done
by our HR department looking purely

00:19:19.630 --> 00:19:23.280
for culture fit, and they have to pass
both in order to be hired.

00:19:23.280 --> 00:19:26.680
So we've passed on a lot of really smart,
talented people that

00:19:26.680 --> 00:19:29.690
we know can make an immediate impact on
our top or bottom line.

00:19:29.690 --> 00:19:32.650
But if they're not good for our culture we
won't hire them.

00:19:32.650 --> 00:19:34.260
And the reverse is true too.

00:19:34.260 --> 00:19:37.730
We'll fire someone if they're bad for our
culture even if they're doing their

00:19:37.730 --> 00:19:40.450
specific job function perfectly fine, even
if

00:19:40.450 --> 00:19:42.820
they're a super star in their job
function.

00:19:42.820 --> 00:19:45.510
If they're bad for the culture, we'll fire
them just for that reason.

00:19:45.510 --> 00:19:48.520
And our performance reviews are 50% based
on whether

00:19:48.520 --> 00:19:52.580
you're living and inspiring the Zappos
culture in others.

00:19:52.580 --> 00:19:55.820
The the other thing we have is something
called a culture book,

00:19:55.820 --> 00:19:59.910
and I'll make that freely available give
you information later on about that.

00:19:59.910 --> 00:20:03.040
But it's something we put out once a year,
and we ask all of

00:20:03.040 --> 00:20:05.230
our employees to write a few paragraphs

00:20:05.230 --> 00:20:07.730
about what the Zappos culture means to
them.

00:20:07.730 --> 00:20:10.220
And except for typos it's unedited, so it
includes

00:20:10.220 --> 00:20:11.750
the good and the bad and it's organized by

00:20:11.750 --> 00:20:13.920
department, so you can kinda see how the
accounting

00:20:13.920 --> 00:20:16.440
culture may be slightly different from the
warehouse culture.

00:20:16.440 --> 00:20:18.900
So kind of like when you go to Amazon and
you read

00:20:18.900 --> 00:20:20.450
customer reviews of products, these are

00:20:20.450 --> 00:20:23.290
essentially employee reviews of the
company.

00:20:23.290 --> 00:20:24.630
We're also very active on Twitter.

00:20:24.630 --> 00:20:29.120
If you go to Twitter.Zappos.com, you can,
see there's a page there.

00:20:29.120 --> 00:20:31.710
We have about 500 employees that are
active on Twitter.

00:20:31.710 --> 00:20:34.520
We introduce every employee to Twitter
during the training, and then

00:20:34.520 --> 00:20:36.900
it's up to them whether they want to
continue using it.

00:20:36.900 --> 00:20:40.940
But then we have a page that aggregates
all of the employee tweets together,

00:20:40.940 --> 00:20:44.380
and so the way that's helped our culture
is, you may not talk to

00:20:44.380 --> 00:20:47.480
that person that's three aisles down from
you, except for saying hi in the

00:20:47.480 --> 00:20:49.580
hallway, but through Twitter, you might
find

00:20:49.580 --> 00:20:51.880
out that he went hiking over the weekend.

00:20:51.880 --> 00:20:53.420
And if you're also an avid hiker, now you
have

00:20:53.420 --> 00:20:56.870
something you can talk about, and you
might go hiking together.

00:20:56.870 --> 00:21:00.270
So those are some of the things that we do
on the company culture side.

00:21:00.270 --> 00:21:03.670
In terms of growing our brand over the
next several years,

00:21:03.670 --> 00:21:06.780
we have what we internally refer to as the
three C's.

00:21:07.940 --> 00:21:11.250
Clothing, customer service, and company
culture.

00:21:11.250 --> 00:21:13.434
And really we think of this as, in terms
of the life

00:21:13.434 --> 00:21:16.346
cycle of the customer, so customer that
have never heard of Zappos

00:21:16.346 --> 00:21:18.634
before, have no idea what we do, we want
them to know

00:21:18.634 --> 00:21:20.090
that we've got a great selection

00:21:20.090 --> 00:21:23.240
of clothing, footwear and other product
categories.

00:21:23.240 --> 00:21:25.683
Once they know about our selection, then
we want them

00:21:25.683 --> 00:21:28.562
to know that we're all about delivering
the very best customer

00:21:28.562 --> 00:21:31.223
service, which isn't something that we
tell them so much

00:21:31.223 --> 00:21:34.209
as something that they experience when
they get that, you know,

00:21:34.209 --> 00:21:36.379
see how easy that free shipping both ways
is, or

00:21:36.379 --> 00:21:39.204
get that surprise upgrade to overnight
shipping or talk to one

00:21:39.204 --> 00:21:41.485
of our customer loyalty reps and see, see
that the

00:21:41.485 --> 00:21:44.369
person on the other end truly wants to
provide great service.

00:21:44.369 --> 00:21:46.118
And once they know that we're all about

00:21:46.118 --> 00:21:48.664
delivering the very best customer service,
then we want

00:21:48.664 --> 00:21:50.890
them to know about our culture, and core
values,

00:21:50.890 --> 00:21:54.090
which is essentially a formalized
definition of our culture.

00:21:54.090 --> 00:21:57.720
Cuz, that's, really, the platform that
makes all of that possible.

00:21:57.720 --> 00:22:00.590
So we've actually had customers tell us
that, when they get that perfect

00:22:00.590 --> 00:22:05.750
outfit or perfect pair of shoes, that
Zappos is happiness in a box.

00:22:05.750 --> 00:22:07.120
So whether it's the happiness that the

00:22:07.120 --> 00:22:09.150
customers feel from getting that perfect
outfit or

00:22:09.150 --> 00:22:10.760
a perfect pair of shoes, or the

00:22:10.760 --> 00:22:13.970
happiness that a customers get, feel from
experiencing

00:22:13.970 --> 00:22:16.480
great customer service, or the happiness
that

00:22:16.480 --> 00:22:18.880
employees feel from being part of a
culture

00:22:18.880 --> 00:22:21.530
where the personal values match their own,
where,

00:22:21.530 --> 00:22:23.840
their personal values match the corporate
core values.

00:22:23.840 --> 00:22:25.520
The thing that we realize ties all these

00:22:25.520 --> 00:22:28.890
things together is that Zappos is really
just about

00:22:28.890 --> 00:22:32.820
delivering happiness whether it's to
customers, employees and we

00:22:32.820 --> 00:22:35.670
apply that same philosophy to our vendors
as well.

00:22:36.710 --> 00:22:39.830
So when you come in for a tour at Zappos
in

00:22:39.830 --> 00:22:43.380
our lobby area, the first thing you'll see
is the Zappos library.

00:22:43.380 --> 00:22:45.150
And there's about 30 or 40 titles there.

00:22:45.150 --> 00:22:47.570
These are two of the titles you'll see.

00:22:47.570 --> 00:22:50.940
Except it's not a lending library, it's a
giving library.

00:22:50.940 --> 00:22:54.720
And we give the books out freely to our
employees as well as

00:22:54.720 --> 00:22:58.430
to visitors, because one of our core
values is to pursue growth and learning.

00:22:58.430 --> 00:23:00.730
So at the end of the tour a lot of
visitors will take home three

00:23:00.730 --> 00:23:03.280
or four titles that they think are
relevant

00:23:03.280 --> 00:23:05.890
to their business or their own personal
life.

00:23:05.890 --> 00:23:08.590
And two of the books that are, some of the
titles rotate in and out,

00:23:08.590 --> 00:23:13.220
two of the books that are always there are
Good to Great and Tribal Leadership.

00:23:13.220 --> 00:23:16.140
And in fact, we have even partnered with
the Office of Tribal Leadership.

00:23:16.140 --> 00:23:17.520
You can download one of the audio versions

00:23:17.520 --> 00:23:20.680
of the book for free from the Zappos
website.

00:23:20.680 --> 00:23:24.620
And we, in fact, teach classes on each of
these books to our employees.

00:23:24.620 --> 00:23:26.550
And the reason why these two books we
really

00:23:26.550 --> 00:23:31.280
believe strongly in is because the authors
researched and

00:23:31.280 --> 00:23:34.040
looked at what separated the great
companies in terms

00:23:34.040 --> 00:23:37.390
of long-term financial performance from
just the good ones.

00:23:37.390 --> 00:23:39.250
And they were actually surprised by their
findings.

00:23:39.250 --> 00:23:40.970
It wasn't at all what they expected.

00:23:40.970 --> 00:23:44.640
They found that the great companies had
two important ingredients that

00:23:44.640 --> 00:23:48.050
the good ones and the mediocre ones
generally did not have.

00:23:48.050 --> 00:23:49.960
And the first important ingredient was
that

00:23:49.960 --> 00:23:53.470
the great companies all had strong
cultures.

00:23:53.470 --> 00:23:57.440
And for us we formalized the definition of
our culture into ten core values.

00:23:57.440 --> 00:24:00.580
And we actually didn't always have core
values at Zappos.

00:24:00.580 --> 00:24:02.420
We'd been around for 11 years, and it
wasn't

00:24:02.420 --> 00:24:04.490
until five or six years into it that we

00:24:04.490 --> 00:24:07.030
rolled out our core values cuz it was
kinda

00:24:07.030 --> 00:24:10.240
something that a bunch of us, myself
personally, resisted.

00:24:10.240 --> 00:24:12.350
Cuz it felt like one of those big
corporate things

00:24:12.350 --> 00:24:14.860
to do and you know, a lot of corporations
have,

00:24:14.860 --> 00:24:17.390
they might call them core values or
guiding principles or

00:24:17.390 --> 00:24:20.890
so on but the problem is usually they're
very lofty sounding.

00:24:20.890 --> 00:24:24.388
They kind of read like a press release the
marketing department put out.

00:24:24.388 --> 00:24:27.818
They sound just like their competitors and
maybe, you learn about it on day

00:24:27.818 --> 00:24:31.940
one of your job but then it becomes this
meaningless plaque on the lobby wall.

00:24:31.940 --> 00:24:34.820
Well, we wanted to come up with
committable core values.

00:24:34.820 --> 00:24:38.840
And by committable, meaning we're willing
to hire or fire people

00:24:38.840 --> 00:24:41.430
based on those values completely

00:24:41.430 --> 00:24:43.080
independent of their actual job
performance.

00:24:44.090 --> 00:24:46.270
And when you use that criteria, it's
actually

00:24:46.270 --> 00:24:47.890
a really hard list to come up with.

00:24:47.890 --> 00:24:49.620
It took us a year to come up with it.

00:24:49.620 --> 00:24:52.379
And it wasn't just a few executives that
spent a long

00:24:52.379 --> 00:24:55.840
weekend at an offsite somewhere and came
up with the core values.

00:24:55.840 --> 00:24:58.480
But instead, I just emailed the entire
company and asked our

00:24:58.480 --> 00:25:01.430
employees what should our values be, got a
whole bunch of different

00:25:01.430 --> 00:25:04.440
responses back and went back and forth for
about a year

00:25:04.440 --> 00:25:07.940
and then eventually came up with our list
of ten core values.

00:25:07.940 --> 00:25:09.290
So, this is our list of ten core

00:25:09.290 --> 00:25:13.330
values and we actually have interview
questions for each

00:25:13.330 --> 00:25:15.500
and everyone of these core values and, and

00:25:15.500 --> 00:25:17.530
actually one of the cool things that I
like

00:25:17.530 --> 00:25:21.500
about the list we ended up with is if you
do a Google search for any one

00:25:21.500 --> 00:25:25.660
of these core values by itself in almost
all

00:25:25.660 --> 00:25:28.860
the cases Zappos is the number one search
result.

00:25:28.860 --> 00:25:33.350
Whereas for take almost any other company
do a search for one of their core

00:25:33.350 --> 00:25:38.030
values and page after page after page you
won't see that company name show up.

00:25:38.030 --> 00:25:40.590
So the one that actually probably trips us
up the

00:25:40.590 --> 00:25:43.105
most during the hiring process is this
last one, be humble.

00:25:43.105 --> 00:25:45.340
Cuz there's a lot of really smart,

00:25:45.340 --> 00:25:48.940
talented people out there that are also
egotistical.

00:25:48.940 --> 00:25:51.130
And for us, it's not a question.

00:25:51.130 --> 00:25:53.850
We just won't hire them, where as the
conversation at most

00:25:53.850 --> 00:25:56.650
other companies would be, well this person
might be kind of

00:25:56.650 --> 00:25:59.680
annoying and rub you the wrong way but
he's gonna add

00:25:59.680 --> 00:26:03.540
a lot of value, and therefore we should
hire that person.

00:26:03.540 --> 00:26:05.450
And that one person may or may not bring

00:26:05.450 --> 00:26:07.520
the company closer downhill, but I think
if you keep

00:26:07.520 --> 00:26:11.210
making compromises like that over and over
and over again,

00:26:11.210 --> 00:26:15.510
that's why most large companies don't have
great company cultures.

00:26:15.510 --> 00:26:19.180
But it's actually probably the one that's
hardest to actually ask a natura

00:26:19.180 --> 00:26:22.310
interview question for it cuz you can't
just say, how humble are you?

00:26:22.310 --> 00:26:24.680
And they say, I'm the most humble person
in the whole wide world.

00:26:24.680 --> 00:26:28.740
[LAUGH] Right and so but one of the ways
we test for

00:26:28.740 --> 00:26:32.630
this is a lot of our candidates actually
are from out of town.

00:26:33.700 --> 00:26:37.090
And so we're picking them up from the
airport in a Zappos shuttle.

00:26:37.090 --> 00:26:40.380
Give them the tour, and then they'll spend
the entire day interviewing.

00:26:40.380 --> 00:26:43.210
Well, at the end of the day of interviews,
the recruiter will

00:26:43.210 --> 00:26:46.110
circle back with the shuttle driver, and
ask how they were treated.

00:26:46.110 --> 00:26:49.820
And it doesn't matter how well the day of
interviews went, if they didn't

00:26:49.820 --> 00:26:54.080
treat the shuttle driver well, then we
won't hire them, it's not even a question.

00:26:54.080 --> 00:26:57.890
So I'll give some examples of other of
interview questions we ask.

00:26:57.890 --> 00:27:00.810
Number three, create fun and a little
weirdness.

00:27:00.810 --> 00:27:05.340
One of our interview questions is actually
on a scale one to 10 how weird are you?

00:27:05.340 --> 00:27:08.130
And you know, if you answer one you might
be

00:27:08.130 --> 00:27:10.800
a little bit too straight laced for the
Zappos culture.

00:27:10.800 --> 00:27:13.640
If you answer 10 you might be to psychotic
for us.

00:27:13.640 --> 00:27:17.130
But it's actually not so much the actual
number we care

00:27:17.130 --> 00:27:21.790
about, our whole belief is that everyone's
a little weird some how.

00:27:21.790 --> 00:27:23.340
And this is really more just a fun way of

00:27:23.340 --> 00:27:28.110
saying that we really recognize and
celebrate each person's individuality.

00:27:28.110 --> 00:27:31.652
And we want their true personality to come
out and shine in the work place.

00:27:31.652 --> 00:27:33.330
You know, there's so many people in

00:27:33.330 --> 00:27:35.570
corporate America where they're a
different person

00:27:35.570 --> 00:27:39.620
at home on weekends versus when they show
up to the office on Monday's.

00:27:39.620 --> 00:27:42.880
And you know they end up leaving a little
part of themselves

00:27:42.880 --> 00:27:45.900
or in a lot of cases, a big part of
themselves at home.

00:27:45.900 --> 00:27:51.180
And that leads to discussions about work
life, separation or work life balance.

00:27:51.180 --> 00:27:53.660
And for us actually rather than worry
about work

00:27:53.660 --> 00:27:55.540
life separation, we really think about it
in terms

00:27:55.540 --> 00:27:58.510
of work life integration, we want the
person to

00:27:58.510 --> 00:28:00.765
be the same person at home or in the
office.

00:28:00.765 --> 00:28:04.330
Cuz what we found is that's when the great
ideas come out, that's when

00:28:04.330 --> 00:28:06.410
their creativity shines and that's when
true

00:28:06.410 --> 00:28:09.600
friendships are formed, not just coworker
relationships.

00:28:09.600 --> 00:28:14.330
And that's really what, when people are in
that environment, that's when the passion

00:28:14.330 --> 00:28:18.570
comes out and that's really what's driven
a lot of our growth over the years.

00:28:18.570 --> 00:28:23.220
And core value number four be adventurous,
creative and open-minded.

00:28:23.220 --> 00:28:25.410
So one of our interview questions here is,
on a

00:28:25.410 --> 00:28:28.750
scale of one to ten, how lucky are you in
life.

00:28:28.750 --> 00:28:32.070
One is, I don't know why bad things always
seem to happen to me,

00:28:32.070 --> 00:28:35.680
and ten is, I don't know why good things
always seem to happen to me.

00:28:35.680 --> 00:28:38.860
Well, we don't want to hire the ones, cuz
they're bad

00:28:38.860 --> 00:28:41.287
luck and we don't want bad luck to come to
Zappos.

00:28:41.287 --> 00:28:44.220
[LAUGH] That, that wouldn't be good.

00:28:44.220 --> 00:28:48.530
No but this was actually inspired by a
research study that I read about several

00:28:48.530 --> 00:28:50.740
years earlier where they actually ask that
exact

00:28:50.740 --> 00:28:53.180
same question to a random group of people.

00:28:53.180 --> 00:28:56.090
And they got, you know, someone, sometimes
a bunch of answers

00:28:56.090 --> 00:29:00.300
in between, and then afterwards, they have
them do a task.

00:29:00.300 --> 00:29:03.530
And the task was to go through a newspaper
and

00:29:03.530 --> 00:29:07.170
count the number of photos that were in
that newspaper.

00:29:07.170 --> 00:29:10.580
But what the participants didn't know was
that it was actually

00:29:10.580 --> 00:29:14.810
a fake newspaper, and sprinkled throughout
the newspaper were these headlines

00:29:14.810 --> 00:29:17.480
that would say things like if you're
reading this now, you

00:29:17.480 --> 00:29:23.330
can stop the answer is 37 plus collect an
extra $100.

00:29:23.330 --> 00:29:26.270
And what they found was that people that
considered

00:29:26.270 --> 00:29:30.610
themselves unlucky in life, generally
never noticed the headlines.

00:29:30.610 --> 00:29:35.625
They went through the task and, you know,
eventually came up with the right answer.

00:29:35.625 --> 00:29:37.750
Whereas the people that considered
themselves lucky in

00:29:37.750 --> 00:29:42.100
life generally stopped early and made the
extra $100.

00:29:42.100 --> 00:29:44.362
So the take away is that it's not so

00:29:44.362 --> 00:29:48.196
much that people are inherently lucky or
unlucky in life.

00:29:48.196 --> 00:29:50.267
But luck is really more about being open
to

00:29:50.267 --> 00:29:53.708
opportunity beyond just how the task or
situation presents itself.

00:29:53.708 --> 00:29:58.274
So that's why we ask that question for
core value number four, be creative.

00:29:58.274 --> 00:30:01.150
Adventures and open-minded.

00:30:01.150 --> 00:30:05.350
And so one of our other core values is
about being open and honest.

00:30:05.350 --> 00:30:08.180
It's really just all about transparency
and we really try to be as

00:30:08.180 --> 00:30:14.000
transparent as possible to our customers,
to our employees to our vendors as well.

00:30:14.000 --> 00:30:18.210
So for our customers, for example, we hold
a quarterly all

00:30:18.210 --> 00:30:21.230
hands meeting for our employees and we
actually live stream that on

00:30:21.230 --> 00:30:24.130
the internet, so anyone can tune in and
employees will ask questions

00:30:24.130 --> 00:30:28.520
about company financials or what brands
we're gonna carry and so on.

00:30:28.520 --> 00:30:31.810
The other thing we do is when there's a
reporter that

00:30:31.810 --> 00:30:34.140
wants to do a story on us, whether it's
for TV

00:30:34.140 --> 00:30:37.290
or a magazine or so on you know most
companies, most

00:30:37.290 --> 00:30:40.600
corporations, what would happen is they're
escorted around by PR person.

00:30:40.600 --> 00:30:43.630
The PR person says you can talk to that VP
over there, and that

00:30:43.630 --> 00:30:45.990
person in communications over there,
everyone else

00:30:45.990 --> 00:30:48.525
is off limits, don't talk to anyone else.

00:30:48.525 --> 00:30:50.360
Whereas what we do is we give a tour and

00:30:50.360 --> 00:30:52.770
at the end of the tour, we say, bathroom's
over

00:30:52.770 --> 00:30:55.380
there, lunch room's over there, walk
around talk to whoever

00:30:55.380 --> 00:30:58.300
you feel like, and when you're done, come
find me.

00:30:58.300 --> 00:31:01.280
And the reason we're comfortable doing
that is because we

00:31:01.280 --> 00:31:04.890
know that employees understand the
long-term vision of the company.

00:31:04.890 --> 00:31:07.920
And we know that employees we've hired,

00:31:07.920 --> 00:31:10.550
their personal values match the corporate
values.

00:31:10.550 --> 00:31:14.480
So every employee is just automatically
living the brand.

00:31:14.480 --> 00:31:16.480
And, you know, they're not going to, we
don't

00:31:16.480 --> 00:31:19.550
do media training and so when the reporter
talks

00:31:19.550 --> 00:31:21.120
to 10 different employees, they're not
gonna get the

00:31:21.120 --> 00:31:24.400
exact same phrases or sound bites and, and
so on.

00:31:24.400 --> 00:31:28.470
But what they are gonna find is
consistency in every

00:31:28.470 --> 00:31:32.440
employee's attitude, and more importantly
the authenticity in their interactions.

00:31:32.440 --> 00:31:36.270
And so that's why we en, we're, we just
tell anyone just walk

00:31:36.270 --> 00:31:38.120
around talk to whoever you, you

00:31:38.120 --> 00:31:40.700
want because that's what we're comfortable
with.

00:31:40.700 --> 00:31:44.390
With our employees we have a monthly
newsletter called Ask Anything where it's

00:31:44.390 --> 00:31:49.430
literally that and employees can ask about
financials or brands or whatever and we'll

00:31:49.430 --> 00:31:51.640
find the best person to answer them and
then put that all in

00:31:51.640 --> 00:31:56.000
a newsletter and we send that out to the
entire company once a month.

00:31:56.000 --> 00:31:59.380
For our vendors, we work with 1500
different brands.

00:31:59.380 --> 00:32:02.350
And they have access to this backend
system we call

00:32:02.350 --> 00:32:05.260
the Extranet where they can log in and
view the exact

00:32:05.260 --> 00:32:08.790
same information our own buyers and
merchandisers can view so they

00:32:08.790 --> 00:32:13.390
can view on hand inventory, sales,
profitability, markdowns and so on.

00:32:13.390 --> 00:32:15.050
And when we first rolled this out the

00:32:15.050 --> 00:32:16.900
response from the vendor community was wow
this

00:32:16.900 --> 00:32:19.170
is great but aren't you worried this
information

00:32:19.170 --> 00:32:21.460
is gonna get into the hands of
competitors?

00:32:21.460 --> 00:32:23.945
And, realistically, I'm sure some of the
information does

00:32:23.945 --> 00:32:26.210
eventually find its way into the hands of
competitors.

00:32:26.210 --> 00:32:30.170
But, on the flip side, we now have an
extra 1500 pairs

00:32:30.170 --> 00:32:34.780
of eyes helping us co-manage our business,
and they're not on our payroll.

00:32:34.780 --> 00:32:38.610
And so for us we found that the benefits
far outweigh any perceived risk.

00:32:38.610 --> 00:32:40.590
Cuz a lot of times, they'll catch stuff
that our own

00:32:40.590 --> 00:32:43.410
buyers or merchandisers might miss if
there's some style that's suddenly

00:32:43.410 --> 00:32:46.570
taking off and, you know, our buyers have
portfolios of 20

00:32:46.570 --> 00:32:48.950
to 30 brands so they may not catch it in
time.

00:32:48.950 --> 00:32:51.780
So, you know, this is a common reaction
get, we get

00:32:51.780 --> 00:32:55.190
sometimes, okay, happy for you Zappos, you
have this great culture.

00:32:55.190 --> 00:32:57.190
Made the Fortune 100 best companies to
work for list but

00:32:57.190 --> 00:33:00.760
the stuff you're talking about would never
work at my company.

00:33:00.760 --> 00:33:05.910
and, when people come to our offices,
they're like, oh, okay, I, I get it but

00:33:05.910 --> 00:33:12.010
the way your, you allow your employees to
do X Y Z, would never fly at my company.

00:33:12.010 --> 00:33:16.289
But what I found really interesting about
the research from good to great in tribal

00:33:16.289 --> 00:33:18.584
leadership, is that they've found that it

00:33:18.584 --> 00:33:21.770
actually doesn't matter what your core
values are.

00:33:21.770 --> 00:33:23.740
What matters, is that you have them.

00:33:23.740 --> 00:33:25.830
And the power you get is out of the
alignment you

00:33:25.830 --> 00:33:30.030
get, when you have the entire organization
acting a certain way.

00:33:30.030 --> 00:33:32.650
And have kind of default way of thinking.

00:33:32.650 --> 00:33:35.700
And, and, and that's what I found really
interesting.

00:33:35.700 --> 00:33:38.420
And for us, what the real power came from

00:33:38.420 --> 00:33:42.000
when the values become integrated into our
everyday language.

00:33:42.000 --> 00:33:44.370
And we don't even notice it but not a day
goes by where we

00:33:44.370 --> 00:33:46.330
don't use our core values just in

00:33:46.330 --> 00:33:49.400
our everyday conversation on a day-to-day
basis.

00:33:49.400 --> 00:33:51.930
So it's really, that's the real power of
having

00:33:51.930 --> 00:33:55.940
a strong culture, of having alignment
throughout the entire organization.

00:33:55.940 --> 00:33:57.705
And I'm gonna make this presentation
available

00:33:57.705 --> 00:34:00.310
cuz another reaction we get sometimes and

00:34:00.310 --> 00:34:03.960
I wanted to include these other stories
is, oh Zappos you're an internet company.

00:34:03.960 --> 00:34:06.640
Special rules apply to internet companies.

00:34:06.640 --> 00:34:09.310
And some of these stories in here are
actually from

00:34:09.310 --> 00:34:11.760
companies in completely different
industries

00:34:11.760 --> 00:34:14.210
and completely different types of
companies.

00:34:14.210 --> 00:34:16.126
And one of the other ways we're
transparent is actually we

00:34:16.126 --> 00:34:20.155
have this separate entity called Zappos
Insights, it's actually it's own website,

00:34:20.155 --> 00:34:24.720
zapposinsights.com, where we, actually
help other companies figure out how to

00:34:24.720 --> 00:34:27.560
build their own strong cultures and figure
out their own core values.

00:34:27.560 --> 00:34:30.460
And there's a monthly video subscription
service, there's

00:34:30.460 --> 00:34:32.770
also these one day and two day seminars

00:34:32.770 --> 00:34:34.710
where people and companies from all over
the

00:34:34.710 --> 00:34:37.710
world actually fly in and go through these
exercises.

00:34:37.710 --> 00:34:41.040
And one of the stories I included in
there, is a company

00:34:41.040 --> 00:34:45.075
that I went through about a year ago,
called The Atlanta Refrigeration Company.

00:34:45.075 --> 00:34:49.200
They're based in Atlanta, Georgia and they
do refrigeration repairs out in the field.

00:34:49.200 --> 00:34:54.270
So in some ways you can't think of a more
opposite company or industry than Zappos.

00:34:54.270 --> 00:34:57.740
And they went back, focused on company
culture, focused

00:34:57.740 --> 00:35:01.310
on delivering better customer service, and
now they're reporting back

00:35:01.310 --> 00:35:04.200
that their customers are happier, their
employees are happier,

00:35:04.200 --> 00:35:06.470
they even sent us before and after
pictures of their

00:35:06.470 --> 00:35:10.400
offices, and in a down economy, their
revenues and

00:35:10.400 --> 00:35:13.140
profits are up, and so for us, it's just
been

00:35:13.140 --> 00:35:15.920
really interesting and rewarding to see
this whole idea

00:35:15.920 --> 00:35:19.300
of happiness as a business model work in
other industries

00:35:19.300 --> 00:35:21.010
and, and other companies.

00:35:21.010 --> 00:35:24.390
So going back to Good to Great and Tribal
Leadership,

00:35:24.390 --> 00:35:27.940
I said the author's researched and they
found there were

00:35:27.940 --> 00:35:30.980
two important ingredients that separated
the great companies, in terms

00:35:30.980 --> 00:35:34.000
of long term financial performance from
just the good ones.

00:35:34.000 --> 00:35:37.620
The first thing ingredient was the great
companies all had strong cultures.

00:35:37.620 --> 00:35:42.810
And the second one is actually really
counter-intuitive at least to me.

00:35:42.810 --> 00:35:44.940
And what they found was that the second

00:35:44.940 --> 00:35:47.930
important ingredient was that these great
companies all had

00:35:47.930 --> 00:35:50.820
a vision that had a higher purpose beyond
just

00:35:50.820 --> 00:35:53.920
money or profits or being number one in
market.

00:35:53.920 --> 00:35:57.970
And the weird thing and ironic thing about
that is, actually by having a

00:35:57.970 --> 00:36:00.920
higher purpose, it actually enabled these
companies

00:36:00.920 --> 00:36:04.390
to generate more profits in the long run.

00:36:04.390 --> 00:36:06.980
And sometimes I'll be speaking at an
entrepreneur

00:36:06.980 --> 00:36:10.100
conference for example and I'll get
approached afterwards where

00:36:10.100 --> 00:36:12.690
entrepreneurs will ask what's a good
market to get

00:36:12.690 --> 00:36:14.890
into, where I can make a lot of money?

00:36:14.890 --> 00:36:18.350
And my advice to them is rather than have
money

00:36:18.350 --> 00:36:21.970
be your primary motivator, instead think
about what would you be

00:36:21.970 --> 00:36:24.980
so passionate about doing that you'd be
happy doing that for

00:36:24.980 --> 00:36:27.480
10 years, even if you never made any money
from it?

00:36:27.480 --> 00:36:29.830
And that's what you should be doing and if
you actually

00:36:29.830 --> 00:36:33.430
do that, it actually, in an ironic way, it
greatly increases

00:36:33.430 --> 00:36:36.800
your chances of making more money in the
long run because

00:36:36.800 --> 00:36:39.150
your passion is what's gonna get you
through the tough times.

00:36:39.150 --> 00:36:42.230
Your passion is gonna rub off on your
employees and

00:36:42.230 --> 00:36:46.130
your your, sup, suppliers and the
customers are gonna sense it.

00:36:46.130 --> 00:36:48.810
And be, the, it has this whole ripple,
domino effect.

00:36:48.810 --> 00:36:52.290
So I like to say chase the vision, not the
money.

00:36:52.290 --> 00:36:56.259
There was a movie that came out several
years ago called Notorious.

00:36:58.180 --> 00:36:59.750
I think it lasted a week in the theater
and

00:36:59.750 --> 00:37:01.920
I may have been the only person that saw
this movie.

00:37:03.570 --> 00:37:09.070
But in the movie Puff Daddy says to rapper
Biggie Smalls

00:37:09.070 --> 00:37:14.250
also known as Notorious B.I.G. don't chase
the paper, chase the dream.

00:37:17.860 --> 00:37:21.872
I just wanted an excuse to put this
picture up here in a business talk, but.

00:37:21.872 --> 00:37:27.660
So if you're an entrepreneur, you know
think about what would you be

00:37:27.660 --> 00:37:30.690
so passionate about doing that you would
be happy doing it for ten years.

00:37:30.690 --> 00:37:32.450
Even if you never made any money from it.

00:37:32.450 --> 00:37:34.280
And that's what you should be doing.

00:37:34.280 --> 00:37:36.470
And if you have employees that report to

00:37:36.470 --> 00:37:39.030
you, then think about what's the higher
vision and

00:37:39.030 --> 00:37:41.050
greater purpose in their work beyond just
money

00:37:41.050 --> 00:37:43.140
or profits or being number one in a
market.

00:37:44.150 --> 00:37:47.700
Cuz that's what's actually gonna help
inspire them.

00:37:47.700 --> 00:37:51.300
And you know, there's a lot of consultants
and books that talk about

00:37:51.300 --> 00:37:55.450
how to motivate employees, and there
always seems to be a new trend in.

00:37:55.450 --> 00:37:57.860
And you know, the latest management fads
and

00:37:57.860 --> 00:38:00.030
you know, there's different ways to
motivate employees.

00:38:00.030 --> 00:38:02.500
They're a lot of corporate in America
actually motivate

00:38:02.500 --> 00:38:06.730
employees through fear you can also
motivate employees through

00:38:06.730 --> 00:38:11.350
incentives and you can motivate employees
through recognition and

00:38:11.350 --> 00:38:14.070
all those things work up to a certain
extent.

00:38:14.070 --> 00:38:20.170
But what we found at Zappos is there's a
huge difference between motivation and

00:38:20.170 --> 00:38:23.420
inspiration, and if you can inspire your

00:38:23.420 --> 00:38:26.210
employees by having a higher purpose in
the

00:38:26.210 --> 00:38:30.230
company beyond just money or profits or
being number one in market in, if you

00:38:30.230 --> 00:38:32.830
can inspire your employees by having
corporate

00:38:32.830 --> 00:38:35.750
core values, that match their own personal
values.

00:38:35.750 --> 00:38:39.660
And not just the stated core values but
the actual practice core values.

00:38:39.660 --> 00:38:42.160
Then you can accomplish so much more and
you don't

00:38:42.160 --> 00:38:44.840
really need to worry about the motivation
part of it.

00:38:44.840 --> 00:38:48.930
The inspiration just will ex, will
accomplish much,

00:38:48.930 --> 00:38:50.900
much more than the motivation part of it.

00:38:50.900 --> 00:38:54.220
So this is the evolution of our brand at
Zappos.

00:38:54.220 --> 00:38:56.820
In 1999, it was just, okay, let's just,
our

00:38:56.820 --> 00:38:58.320
visions was let's just sell a lot of
shoes.

00:38:58.320 --> 00:39:00.360
It's all gonna be about selection.

00:39:00.360 --> 00:39:03.990
And then 4 years into it, in 2003, we all
sat around

00:39:03.990 --> 00:39:08.470
and asked ourselves, what do we want to be
when we grow up?

00:39:08.470 --> 00:39:12.550
Do we wanna be about shoes, or do we wanna
be about something more meaningful?

00:39:12.550 --> 00:39:15.280
And that's when we decided, okay, let's
build the Zappos brand

00:39:15.280 --> 00:39:18.820
to be about the very best customer service
and customer experience.

00:39:18.820 --> 00:39:22.380
And a funny thing happened when we
communicated this to our employees.

00:39:22.380 --> 00:39:26.480
We found that suddenly employees were a
lot more passionate and

00:39:26.480 --> 00:39:30.470
excited about the company and when
customers called they could sense

00:39:30.470 --> 00:39:32.660
the person on the other end of the line
truly wanted

00:39:32.660 --> 00:39:35.750
to provide great service and wasn't there
just for a paycheck.

00:39:35.750 --> 00:39:37.820
We found that when vendors came and
visited us they

00:39:37.820 --> 00:39:40.590
wanted to visit us more often and stay
longer and

00:39:40.590 --> 00:39:43.680
so all these things had this kind of
multiplicated snowball

00:39:43.680 --> 00:39:46.880
effect that drove a lot of our growth over
the years.

00:39:46.880 --> 00:39:50.200
And in 2005 was when we switched from
culture to just

00:39:50.200 --> 00:39:54.050
being important to culture being the
number one priority in the company.

00:39:54.050 --> 00:39:57.490
With the belief that if we get the culture
right, then most of the other stuff,

00:39:57.490 --> 00:40:00.590
delivering great customer service,
building a long-term enduring

00:40:00.590 --> 00:40:03.400
brand that will just take care of itself.

00:40:03.400 --> 00:40:05.850
And then 2007 we started thinking about,
okay, what

00:40:05.850 --> 00:40:08.570
are different ways we can provide great
customer service.

00:40:08.570 --> 00:40:12.640
Some companies focus more on the
technology and the efficiency part of it.

00:40:12.640 --> 00:40:14.980
Well, we decided instead of going the
high-tech approach to go

00:40:14.980 --> 00:40:16.600
the high-touch approach and really

00:40:16.600 --> 00:40:20.040
focus on building personal, emotional
connections.

00:40:20.040 --> 00:40:21.830
And then 2009, we took a step back and

00:40:21.830 --> 00:40:25.390
realized, okay, customer service is about
making customers happy.

00:40:25.390 --> 00:40:28.120
Company culture is about making employees
happy.

00:40:28.120 --> 00:40:30.730
Let's just tie it all together and really
have

00:40:30.730 --> 00:40:33.090
our vision be about delivering happiness
to the world.

00:40:33.090 --> 00:40:36.170
And then from that expanding vision,
that's what led to

00:40:36.170 --> 00:40:40.020
the development of Zappoinsights.com,
which is a completely different business.

00:40:40.020 --> 00:40:43.639
It doesn't have anything to do with
e-commerce or selling shoes online.

00:40:44.890 --> 00:40:47.680
So I wanted to tell another piece of story
and

00:40:47.680 --> 00:40:52.800
this actually happened a few years ago in
Santa Monica California.

00:40:52.800 --> 00:40:57.190
And I was actually at a Sketcher's
conference and it's one

00:40:57.190 --> 00:41:00.050
of the brands we work with, one of the
footwear brands.

00:41:00.050 --> 00:41:01.880
And it was a long day and at the end of

00:41:01.880 --> 00:41:04.580
the day a bunch of us decided to go bar
hopping.

00:41:04.580 --> 00:41:06.340
There were three people from Sketchers

00:41:06.340 --> 00:41:09.520
and three people from Zappos, including
myself.

00:41:09.520 --> 00:41:12.203
I'd never been bar hopping in the Santa
Monica area before.

00:41:12.203 --> 00:41:16.290
So we went to the first bar and some,
someone ordered a round of

00:41:16.290 --> 00:41:21.090
drinks and then someone, I can't remember
who, decided to order a round of shots.

00:41:21.090 --> 00:41:25.890
So been a long day, we took the shots,
finished the round of drinks.

00:41:25.890 --> 00:41:28.780
And when we went to the second bar, and
someone else ordered a round

00:41:28.780 --> 00:41:31.610
of drinks to pay back for that first round
of drinks, and someone else

00:41:31.610 --> 00:41:33.910
ordered a second round of shots to pay
back for that first round of

00:41:33.910 --> 00:41:38.530
shots, and were looking at it, and we
determined that, you can't waste alcohol.

00:41:38.530 --> 00:41:45.290
So we took the shots and finished the
drinks, and then went to the third bar.

00:41:46.950 --> 00:41:50.360
And I'm actually unclear on how many shots
or drinks we had after that.

00:41:50.360 --> 00:41:53.600
But what I do know is that in California,
last

00:41:53.600 --> 00:41:56.665
call is, I think it's two, is it two am?

00:41:56.665 --> 00:42:00.775
I, I'm from Vegas, so last call isn't in
the dictionary there

00:42:00.775 --> 00:42:05.490
but so it's two am and anyways so finally
lights go on.

00:42:05.490 --> 00:42:06.830
&gt;&gt; I don't know how many bars we've been

00:42:06.830 --> 00:42:09.150
to and finally start walking back to the
hotel room.

00:42:09.150 --> 00:42:12.350
And as we were walking back to the hotel
room, one of the

00:42:12.350 --> 00:42:16.370
girls from Sketchers asked if we all
wanted to share a pepperoni pizza.

00:42:16.370 --> 00:42:17.540
And she was so excited.

00:42:17.540 --> 00:42:18.260
And we're like, yeah, sure.

00:42:18.260 --> 00:42:19.770
And she's like, oh, I'm so excited.

00:42:19.770 --> 00:42:23.000
I actually checked it out on the room
service menu before we left.

00:42:23.000 --> 00:42:25.510
It was on page 17, item number two.

00:42:25.510 --> 00:42:27.540
And I know when it comes, it's going to be
super

00:42:27.540 --> 00:42:29.170
hot, and I don't want to burn the roof of
my mouth.

00:42:29.170 --> 00:42:33.200
So I'm just gonna blow on it and let the
aroma slowly waft into my nose.

00:42:33.200 --> 00:42:35.380
And she kept talking about it, and getting
super excited.

00:42:35.380 --> 00:42:37.920
And it was only a five minute walk, but it
seemed much

00:42:37.920 --> 00:42:42.060
longer than that cuz she would not stop
talking about this pepperoni pizza.

00:42:42.060 --> 00:42:45.270
So anyways we finally wind up in someone's
hotel room.

00:42:45.270 --> 00:42:50.640
She's super excited, calls room service
and then ten seconds later

00:42:50.640 --> 00:42:56.250
hangs up the phone all dejected and I ask
her what's wrong

00:42:56.250 --> 00:43:01.300
and she said apparently this hotel doesn't
deliver hot food after 11 p.m.

00:43:01.300 --> 00:43:07.950
And she was like, oh, you have no idea how
much I was craving that pepperoni pizza.

00:43:07.950 --> 00:43:11.055
And I was like, I think we all have a
pretty good idea.

00:43:11.055 --> 00:43:14.390
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:43:14.390 --> 00:43:16.760
&gt;&gt; And, but she was still really sad and
I'm trying to

00:43:16.760 --> 00:43:18.840
think okay, how do you cheer her up and
then I tell

00:43:18.840 --> 00:43:21.510
her the story of how in college I used to
make pizza

00:43:21.510 --> 00:43:25.110
and this guy named Alfred, and, and this
is how we met.

00:43:25.110 --> 00:43:26.570
And at the end of my story she looks at

00:43:26.570 --> 00:43:29.426
me and she's like, that's so not helpful
right now.

00:43:29.426 --> 00:43:34.160
[LAUGH] So you know, Fred, whose also from
Zappos, he and I are,

00:43:34.160 --> 00:43:37.940
are kind of brainstorming and then we come
up with the best idea ever.

00:43:37.940 --> 00:43:40.550
We say, oh call Zappos, call Zappos.

00:43:40.550 --> 00:43:41.648
We're all about the best customer service.

00:43:41.648 --> 00:43:42.250
We'll take care of you.

00:43:42.250 --> 00:43:45.390
And really in our inebriated state of mind

00:43:45.390 --> 00:43:47.610
we thought that was the funniest thing
ever.

00:43:47.610 --> 00:43:48.216
And so.

00:43:48.216 --> 00:43:54.392
[LAUGH] So she actually takes us up on our
dare, and you know, puts it on speaker

00:43:54.392 --> 00:43:58.061
phone and you know, it says, rep answers,

00:43:58.061 --> 00:44:01.990
thanks for calling Zappos, how can I help
you?

00:44:01.990 --> 00:44:05.630
The rest of us are in the background
trying like to be quiet, and I laugh.

00:44:05.630 --> 00:44:07.910
She's like oh, thank goodness, you
answered.

00:44:07.910 --> 00:44:10.220
I know it's three A.M. but I'm in Santa
Monica right

00:44:10.220 --> 00:44:12.435
now in this hotel that doesn't serve hot
food after 11

00:44:12.435 --> 00:44:15.100
P.M. I've been craving this pepperoni
pizza on the room service

00:44:15.100 --> 00:44:17.622
menu, I'm looking at it right now, page 17
item number 2.

00:44:17.622 --> 00:44:20.100
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Is there anything you can do for me?

00:44:22.380 --> 00:44:24.638
Well first, there was an awkward silence.

00:44:24.638 --> 00:44:25.781
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:44:25.781 --> 00:44:29.260
&gt;&gt; And then, and the rep said you know you
called Zappos, right?

00:44:29.260 --> 00:44:32.620
[LAUGHING] We sell shoes.

00:44:32.620 --> 00:44:34.190
We sell clothes.

00:44:34.190 --> 00:44:37.980
But we don't sale pizza yet and she's like
I

00:44:37.980 --> 00:44:40.910
know but I heard you're all about the best
customer service.

00:44:40.910 --> 00:44:43.760
And the rep said okay hold on.

00:44:43.760 --> 00:44:46.250
And put us on hold for two minutes.

00:44:46.250 --> 00:44:49.476
And then came back listing the five
closest places in the Santa

00:44:49.476 --> 00:44:53.026
Monica area that were still open and
delivering pizza at that hour.

00:44:53.026 --> 00:44:55.723
Now, well first of all, I, I hesitate a
little to tell

00:44:55.723 --> 00:44:58.850
this story cuz I don't want all of you to
start calling Zappos.

00:44:58.850 --> 00:44:59.629
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:44:59.629 --> 00:45:02.449
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] Ordering pizza from us.

00:45:02.449 --> 00:45:05.306
But you know, clearly we don't have a
process

00:45:05.306 --> 00:45:09.190
and procedure for late night drunk pizza
orders, but in,

00:45:09.190 --> 00:45:11.607
it just goes back to, I think it's just

00:45:11.607 --> 00:45:14.464
a fun, sort of, illustrate that if you get
the

00:45:14.464 --> 00:45:17.469
culture right, then most of the other
stuff like

00:45:17.469 --> 00:45:22.016
delivering great customer service, and
building your brand, one story

00:45:22.016 --> 00:45:24.359
at a time, one phone call at a time just

00:45:24.359 --> 00:45:27.731
happens naturally on its own and you know,
these types

00:45:27.731 --> 00:45:30.149
of stories we're creating at Zappos,

00:45:30.149 --> 00:45:33.008
literally, thousands and thousands of
times

00:45:33.008 --> 00:45:37.800
every single day and that's how we built
our brand over the years.

00:45:37.800 --> 00:45:40.080
And these are, you know, questions that we
think about.

00:45:40.080 --> 00:45:44.900
How do we create more happy memories for
our customers and how do we elicit

00:45:44.900 --> 00:45:47.020
more positive emotions and really we're in

00:45:47.020 --> 00:45:50.710
the memories, and emotion business and
stories business.

00:45:50.710 --> 00:45:52.140
And then we also think about how do we

00:45:52.140 --> 00:45:55.520
keep thinking differently and expanding
what business we're actually in.

00:45:55.520 --> 00:45:56.700
How do we think bigger and think

00:45:56.700 --> 00:45:58.390
differently, and one of my favorite
stories

00:45:58.390 --> 00:46:00.180
is about Cirque du Soleil where you

00:46:00.180 --> 00:46:02.560
know, they completely redefined the circus
business.

00:46:02.560 --> 00:46:07.770
Prior to Cirque du Soleil, to have a
better circus meant more elephants.

00:46:07.770 --> 00:46:08.520
Bigger elephants.

00:46:08.520 --> 00:46:11.060
And the market research surveys, if they
did

00:46:11.060 --> 00:46:13.830
them, would be exiting you know, doing
exit polls.

00:46:13.830 --> 00:46:15.910
And they'd be like, were the elephants

00:46:15.910 --> 00:46:19.370
of sufficient quantity and sufficient size
and

00:46:19.370 --> 00:46:22.042
you know, and then, that, that would tell
them how to build a better circus.

00:46:22.042 --> 00:46:25.970
And then Cirque du Soleil just thought
completely, you know, outside of the box.

00:46:25.970 --> 00:46:30.110
So anyways just wanted to wrap up and
actually

00:46:30.110 --> 00:46:32.250
take a step back from all of this business
talking.

00:46:32.250 --> 00:46:35.510
How do you guys actually think about this
for yourselves?

00:46:35.510 --> 00:46:37.799
What is your goal in life?

00:46:39.000 --> 00:46:43.380
And like think, for yourself personally
what is your actual goal in life.

00:46:43.380 --> 00:46:45.300
And when I asked different people this
question, I

00:46:45.300 --> 00:46:47.526
actually got a whole bunch of different
answers back.

00:46:47.526 --> 00:46:49.054
Some people would say they wanna

00:46:49.054 --> 00:46:52.180
grow a company, especially at entrepreneur
conferences.

00:46:52.180 --> 00:46:56.270
Some people say they wanna be healthy and
then I'll ask them why.

00:46:56.270 --> 00:46:59.940
So whatever your goal in life is, ask
yourself why.

00:46:59.940 --> 00:47:01.940
And then people come up with another set
of answers.

00:47:01.940 --> 00:47:05.110
They want to retire early or run faster.

00:47:05.110 --> 00:47:07.050
And then I'll ask them why again, and

00:47:07.050 --> 00:47:09.660
so whatever your answer is, just ask
yourself why.

00:47:09.660 --> 00:47:11.930
And what's interesting is if you ask why
enough

00:47:11.930 --> 00:47:14.736
times, almost everyone comes up with the
same answer.

00:47:14.736 --> 00:47:17.740
And it's, they're pursuing their goal in
life

00:47:17.740 --> 00:47:21.110
is that they believe it ultimately make
them happier.

00:47:21.110 --> 00:47:24.700
So I started a few years ago reading about

00:47:24.700 --> 00:47:27.870
this whole field of research called the
Science of Happiness.

00:47:27.870 --> 00:47:29.900
The official name is actually positive
psychology, but

00:47:29.900 --> 00:47:32.130
it's based on actual research that's been
done.

00:47:32.130 --> 00:47:34.650
Not, so I am not saying, talking about go
to, you

00:47:34.650 --> 00:47:37.820
know, self-help session or books or think
positive and you'll be happy.

00:47:37.820 --> 00:47:40.860
But actual research that's been done, and
as I

00:47:40.860 --> 00:47:43.410
was reading about this, prior to 1998 most
of the

00:47:43.410 --> 00:47:46.130
psychology was about how do you take
people that

00:47:46.130 --> 00:47:49.030
have something wrong with them and make
them more normal?

00:47:49.030 --> 00:47:53.760
But almost nobody bothered to study how to
take normal people and make them happier.

00:47:53.760 --> 00:47:55.630
So, as I was reading about this, one

00:47:55.630 --> 00:47:58.210
of the consistent findings from the
research was that

00:47:58.210 --> 00:47:59.860
people are actually very bad at predicting
what

00:47:59.860 --> 00:48:03.170
will make them happy on a sustainab,
sustainable basis.

00:48:03.170 --> 00:48:05.040
Mo peop, most people think, oh once I get
x, then

00:48:05.040 --> 00:48:07.760
I'll be happy, once I achieve x, then
they'll be happy.

00:48:07.760 --> 00:48:10.160
When the research shows, you know, for
example, lottery winners.

00:48:10.160 --> 00:48:12.040
Look at their happiness right before
winning

00:48:12.040 --> 00:48:13.340
the lottery, and then a year later.

00:48:13.340 --> 00:48:16.430
And a year later, it's the same, or maybe
a little bit lower than before.

00:48:16.430 --> 00:48:19.410
So people are bad at predicting what will
make them happy.

00:48:19.410 --> 00:48:21.709
And then, you know, started thinking about
well

00:48:21.709 --> 00:48:23.898
you know, at Zappos there's a science
behind a

00:48:23.898 --> 00:48:25.429
lot of the stuff that we do in terms

00:48:25.429 --> 00:48:27.017
of website conversion, customer

00:48:27.017 --> 00:48:30.210
acquisition, metrics, repeat customer
behavior.

00:48:30.210 --> 00:48:32.475
And if the ultimate goal is happiness,
what if some percent

00:48:32.475 --> 00:48:34.892
of the time was actually spent just
reading up on the research.

00:48:34.892 --> 00:48:35.690
That's been done.

00:48:35.690 --> 00:48:38.805
How can that not only help personally but
how can it help

00:48:38.805 --> 00:48:43.600
the company in terms of making customers
happy and making employees happy.

00:48:43.600 --> 00:48:45.310
Because it's not as simple as just asking

00:48:45.310 --> 00:48:47.740
customers or employees what would make you
happy.

00:48:48.790 --> 00:48:50.460
You know, a lot of people go through life,

00:48:50.460 --> 00:48:53.270
trying to get to that ultimate destination
of happiness.

00:48:53.270 --> 00:48:56.510
But, a lot of people never get there, and
of the people that do get

00:48:56.510 --> 00:48:59.680
there, a lot of them, most of them, find
that it's actually very short lived.

00:48:59.680 --> 00:49:02.360
Not quite what they were expecting.

00:49:02.360 --> 00:49:03.840
What if by reading up on the science

00:49:03.840 --> 00:49:05.330
of happiness and learning a little bit
about the

00:49:05.330 --> 00:49:07.310
research, you can kind of short cut some

00:49:07.310 --> 00:49:10.210
of it and go, to the happiness much
faster.

00:49:10.210 --> 00:49:13.270
So I just wanted to share some of the
frameworks of happiness

00:49:13.270 --> 00:49:17.410
that I thought were most interesting from
the research that I read.

00:49:17.410 --> 00:49:21.470
First framework is happiness is really
just about four things.

00:49:21.470 --> 00:49:24.970
Perceived control, perceived progress,
connectedness,

00:49:24.970 --> 00:49:26.760
meaning the number and depth

00:49:26.760 --> 00:49:30.790
of your relationships, and being part of
something bigger than yourself.

00:49:30.790 --> 00:49:33.170
And what's interesting is you can apply
this to business as well.

00:49:33.170 --> 00:49:35.730
You know, connectedness goes back to
company culture.

00:49:35.730 --> 00:49:38.870
I'll give a quick example for perceived
progress.

00:49:38.870 --> 00:49:42.000
We used to hire people in our
merchandising department at

00:49:42.000 --> 00:49:46.280
entry level, and then give them promotions
every 18 months.

00:49:46.280 --> 00:49:47.690
And you know they get trained and
certified and

00:49:47.690 --> 00:49:50.670
so on, and in three years they become a
buyer.

00:49:50.670 --> 00:49:53.400
Well we changed that a few years ago so
that instead of

00:49:53.400 --> 00:49:59.080
a promotion every 18 months, we gave them
smaller promotions every six months.

00:49:59.080 --> 00:50:02.160
Nothing changed, they, they, they still
took three years to become a buyer.

00:50:02.160 --> 00:50:04.930
They still had to go get certified and so
on, but we found

00:50:04.930 --> 00:50:08.790
employees were much happier cuz there was
that ongoing sense of perceived progress.

00:50:08.790 --> 00:50:10.620
And it cost the company nothing to do
that.

00:50:12.290 --> 00:50:15.142
Maslow's Hierarchy, there's a book called
Peak by Chip Conley, P -

00:50:15.142 --> 00:50:18.110
E - A - K, where he condenses it down to
three levels.

00:50:18.110 --> 00:50:20.500
And so for example for employees, its do
they think

00:50:20.500 --> 00:50:24.080
of their work as a job or a career or
calling?

00:50:24.080 --> 00:50:26.420
And our whole goal at Zappos is to move
them up that

00:50:26.420 --> 00:50:30.480
pyramid so that they're still employees at
Zappos ten years from now.

00:50:30.480 --> 00:50:31.850
And then the last framework I wanted to

00:50:31.850 --> 00:50:34.810
share real quickly are the three types of
happiness.

00:50:34.810 --> 00:50:36.440
Pleasure, engagement and meaning.

00:50:36.440 --> 00:50:39.765
And the first type, I like to call the
rock star type of happiness.

00:50:39.765 --> 00:50:42.250
Cuz it's all about chasing that next high.

00:50:42.250 --> 00:50:45.190
And it's great if you can sustain it, the
problem is

00:50:45.190 --> 00:50:49.660
it's very hard to sustain, unless you're
basically a rock star.

00:50:49.660 --> 00:50:52.200
And what the research has shown is that as
soon

00:50:52.200 --> 00:50:54.310
as the source of stimuli goes away that's
giving you that

00:50:54.310 --> 00:50:57.060
high, as soon as that goes away, your
happiness just

00:50:57.060 --> 00:50:59.940
plummets and drops right down back to
wherever it was before.

00:51:00.970 --> 00:51:02.920
It's the shortest last, lasting type of
happiness.

00:51:02.920 --> 00:51:06.100
The second is called flow, and it's about
we've all experienced this, it's

00:51:06.100 --> 00:51:07.760
about those times where time just flies

00:51:07.760 --> 00:51:09.310
because you're so into whatever you're
doing.

00:51:09.310 --> 00:51:12.550
For some people it's running, for other
people it might be painting.

00:51:12.550 --> 00:51:14.468
And it's three hours passed.

00:51:14.468 --> 00:51:16.080
Seems like only twenty minutes have
passed.

00:51:16.080 --> 00:51:18.260
And professional athletes refer to it as
being

00:51:18.260 --> 00:51:21.460
in the zone, where peak engagement meets
peak performance

00:51:21.460 --> 00:51:24.090
and other characteristics associated with
it are you

00:51:24.090 --> 00:51:26.800
lose the sense of self conscious, self
consciousness or

00:51:26.800 --> 00:51:30.170
even self, and basically the strategy
there is

00:51:30.170 --> 00:51:32.850
notice when it happens and then change
your environment,

00:51:32.850 --> 00:51:35.030
your friends, where you live, your job,
and,

00:51:35.030 --> 00:51:38.000
or so on, to have it happen more often.

00:51:38.000 --> 00:51:43.130
And the research show that, that's the
second longest lasting type of happiness.

00:51:43.130 --> 00:51:44.860
And the third type is the longest lasting
type.

00:51:44.860 --> 00:51:46.670
It's about being part of something bigger
than yourself.

00:51:46.670 --> 00:51:50.920
And for some people might be, volunteering
for your favorite charity for example.

00:51:50.920 --> 00:51:52.870
And what I found interesting is most
people go through

00:51:52.870 --> 00:51:56.360
life chasing after the first type of
happiness, thinking once

00:51:56.360 --> 00:51:58.700
I can sustain that on an ongoing basis,
which is

00:51:58.700 --> 00:52:00.930
next to impossible, then I'll worry about
the second type.

00:52:00.930 --> 00:52:04.670
And then if I ever get around to it, then
I'll worry about the third type.

00:52:04.670 --> 00:52:07.900
When based on the research data, and
purely on the

00:52:07.900 --> 00:52:10.260
research data, the proper strategy is
figure out the third type

00:52:10.260 --> 00:52:13.480
first, layer on top of that the second
type, and then

00:52:13.480 --> 00:52:17.340
anytime you experience the first type,
just icing on the cake.

00:52:17.340 --> 00:52:19.130
So some books I would recommend for

00:52:19.130 --> 00:52:24.170
a copy of this presentation, email
Tony@deliveringhappinessbook.com.

